{
  "title": "Guard clauses and early returns",
  "summary": "Flatten nested ifs by handling the special cases first and leaving early.",
  "minutes": 11,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "The arrow of doom",
      "body": [
        "```csharp",
        "string Ship(Order? order)",
        "{",
        "    if (order != null)",
        "    {",
        "        if (order.IsPaid)",
        "        {",
        "            if (order.Items.Count > 0)",
        "            {",
        "                return \"shipped\";",
        "            }",
        "            else return \"empty\";",
        "        }",
        "        else return \"unpaid\";",
        "    }",
        "    else return \"missing\";",
        "}",
        "```",
        "",
        "The real work is buried at the deepest indent, and each `else` is far from its `if`. The shape of the code points right like an arrow, and every new rule pushes it further."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "mcq",
      "prompt": "In this code, which `if` does `else return \"unpaid\";` belong to?",
      "code": [
        "string Ship(Order? order)",
        "{",
        "    if (order != null)",
        "    {",
        "        if (order.IsPaid)",
        "        {",
        "            if (order.Items.Count > 0)",
        "            {",
        "                return \"shipped\";",
        "            }",
        "            else return \"empty\";",
        "        }",
        "        else return \"unpaid\";",
        "    }",
        "    else return \"missing\";",
        "}"
      ],
      "options": [
        "`if (order.IsPaid)`",
        "`if (order != null)`",
        "`if (order.Items.Count > 0)`",
        "None, it is a syntax error"
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explain": "You had to count braces to answer. That effort, on every read, is the cost of deep nesting."
    },
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Guard clauses",
      "body": [
        "A **guard clause** checks a special case at the top and leaves immediately. Each one removes a level of nesting.",
        "",
        "```csharp",
        "string Ship(Order? order)",
        "{",
        "    if (order == null) return \"missing\";",
        "    if (!order.IsPaid) return \"unpaid\";",
        "    if (order.Items.Count == 0) return \"empty\";",
        "",
        "    return \"shipped\";",
        "}",
        "```",
        "",
        "Same behaviour. Now the method reads top to bottom: reject the bad cases, then do the main thing with no indentation at all. Step through it below to watch each guard get checked. Guards also work with `throw` for arguments that should never be invalid."
      ],
      "trace": {
        "code": [
          "string Ship(bool exists, bool paid, int items)",
          "{",
          "    if (!exists) return \"missing\";",
          "    if (!paid) return \"unpaid\";",
          "    if (items == 0) return \"empty\";",
          "    return \"shipped\";",
          "}",
          "var result = Ship(true, true, 0);",
          "Console.WriteLine(result);"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
